    "confidence": 0.25,
    "explanation": "To the rider and the driver: this case cannot be decided on the evidence, so it goes to a human reviewer. The SGD 6.00 no-show fee stays on hold — it is not taken from the rider and not refunded to her — until that review is finished. Nothing is paid out today.\n\nWhat the case turns on. A no-show fee stands only if the driver completed the full required hold at the pickup point — 480 seconds (8 minutes) for an airport kerb such as Changi T1 Door 6 — measured from the first GPS fix inside the pickup zone, not from the arrival tap. Two records speak to that and they contradict each other. The trip record puts the arrival marker at 23:42:10 and the hold at 6.9 minutes (415 seconds), already short of 480. The GPS trace's first qualifying fix inside the zone is 23:42:21 and its longest unbroken run of qualifying in-zone fixes is 255 seconds — also short — but the driver's device clock runs 228 seconds ahead of the server clock, and applying that correction puts the same entry at 23:38:33 and the hold at 632 seconds, comfortably over. A 186-second gap in the trace (23:39:15 to 23:42:21) sits exactly across the moment of entry and hides which reading is right.\n\nWhy this is an escalation and not a refund. POL-NS-08(a) makes a clock skew over 90 seconds an unsafe match between the arrival marker and any GPS fix, so POL-NS-01 limb (b) is neither proved nor disproved. POL-NS-08(b) makes this trace's 186-second gap, sub-90% coverage and absent rider track fatal to any precise dwell figure, so limb (c), and limb (d) which depends on it, are undecided. The deciding facts therefore rest only on impaired artifacts: the GPS trace (weight 0.25 — CLOCK_SKEW, GPS_GAP, LOW_COVERAGE, TRUNCATED_LOG, LOW_SAMPLE_RATE, PARTIAL_RECORD) and the trip record (weight 0.4 — CROSS_SOURCE_CONFLICT). The two records give conflicting values rather than the same fact, so the cap is the lower of them, 0.25, below the 0.70 floor — POL-NS-09(a). The two admissible records also contradict each other directly on arrival and dwell with no clause able to settle which is right — POL-NS-09(b). And the sole evidence for the deciding fact is impaired under POL-NS-08(a) and (b) — POL-NS-09(e).\n\nOn the individual points. Driver: your proximity argument (nearest approach 96 m, five qualifying in-zone fixes) is read off the same broken trace; it shows you were in the vicinity but cannot fix the entry moment that limb (b) actually reads. I accept that no boarding occurred and that the trip was closed with reason code RIDER_NO_SHOW, one of the three codes limb (d) recognises, but limb (d) also requires the cancellation to fall at or after the elapse of the required hold, which is the very thing left undecided. Your profile shows 2 no-show terminations in 30 days against the threshold of 4 and a rate of 0.013 against 0.08, so the POL-NS-07 anti-farming guard does not trip and nothing withholds your pay — but the 80% compensation (SGD 4.80) cannot be awarded while the hold that earns it is unproved. Rider: I have not found that you were at Door 6; your account is an account, not a measurement, and the trace carries no rider track to test your presence. POL-NS-04 does not exonerate you: your only message on the export is timed 23:49:40, after the 23:49:05 cancellation, so limb (a) is not met, and with no rider track limb (c) is undecidable — 'undecidable' is not 'satisfied'. Your shortfall case is also not established: the 255-second run and the face-value 404-second span to cancellation are both short of 480, but they come from the impaired trace, and the skew-corrected reading is over. The trip record's own 6.9 minutes likewise falls short of 480 seconds, but it runs from the contested arrival marker and is a competing account, not an independent measurement. No traffic advisory covers this kerb (EV-ADVIS-00210-01 is MISSING, weight 0.0), so no interval can be summed to extend the hold; its absence decides nothing in either direction. The itemised charge itself is correctly computed — the greater of the AIRPORT zone fee (SGD 6.00) and the RYDE_X tier fee (SGD 4.00) — so there is no fare-integrity defect, and no clause here produces a referral (POL-NS-05(ii) is not triggered, because qualifying in-zone fixes do exist).\n\nWhat the reviewer must settle. Determine the true in-geofence entry time — in particular whether the 228-second device clock skew should be applied — and therefore whether the driver held at Door 6 for the full 480 seconds. If the hold is verified the fee stands and the driver is paid 80% of it (SGD 4.80); if it is not, the fee is refunded in full to the rider with no driver compensation.\n\nFour claims were discarded at the citation stage, two from each side; the audit record accompanies this ruling."
  },
  "discarded_claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Fare integrity is not in issue, and the charge as itemised is correct. Under the fee schedule's resolution rule the chargeable cancellation fee is the GREATER of the zone-class fee and the vehicle-tier fee: AIRPORT is SGD 6.00 and RYDE_X is SGD 4.00, and the greater is exactly the SGD 6.00 itemised in fare_breakdown.cancellation_fee. POL-NS-08(d) is therefore not engaged, and EV-FARE-00210 — COMPLETE at weight 1.0 — reconciles precisely with this trip's zone class and vehicle tier.",
      "side": "DRIVER",
      "reason": "POL-GEN-02(1) non-disclosure. The claim rests on EV-TRIP-00210 for the trip's zone class and vehicle tier — the very inputs its reconciliation turns on — and EV-TRIP-00210 is IMPAIRED at weight_multiplier 0.40 on CROSS_SOURCE_CONFLICT. The claim names only the fare artifact's clean weight and is silent on the impaired one and its issue code."
    },
    {
      "claim": "EV-TRIP-00210 and EV-GPS-00210 are two admissible artifacts that contradict each other directly on arrival and on dwell — 23:42:10 with a 6.9-minute wait on one side, a first qualifying in-geofence fix at 23:42:21 with a 255-second qualifying run and 22.8% coverage on the other — and no clause in this policy resolves which is right. The sole evidence for the deciding fact is impaired under POL-NS-08 limbs (a) and (b), which on that clause's own terms caps the ruling and sends the case to a reviewer under POL-NS-09. Escalation holds the SGD 6.00 fee on its present PENDING status rather than refunding it, which is the disposition the evidence actually supports.",
      "side": "DRIVER",
      "reason": "POL-GEN-02(1) non-disclosure. The claim relies on EV-GPS-00210 (weight_multiplier 0.25) and EV-TRIP-00210 (0.40) as the sole evidence of the deciding fact but names neither weight nor any issue code, saying only that the artifacts are 'impaired under POL-NS-08 limbs (a) and (b)'. Clause limbs are not issue codes; the required disclosure of impairment and of the codes that fired is absent."
    },
    {
      "claim": "POL-NS-01 limb (d) fails on its own timing conjunct as well. The limb requires the trip to be terminated 'at or after the moment the required dwell elapsed', and limb (c) fixes the start of that dwell at the first qualifying in-geofence fix, not at the arrived tap. The first qualifying in-geofence fix on the trace is 23:42:21 and the trip was cancelled at 23:49:05, 404 seconds later - the driver pulled the plug 76 seconds before the 480th second. A cancellation that precedes the elapse of the required dwell cannot satisfy a limb written to require the opposite.",
      "side": "RIDER",
      "reason": "POL-GEN-02(1) non-disclosure. The claim cites EV-GPS-00210 (weight_multiplier 0.25, IMPAIRED) and EV-TRIP-00210 (0.40, IMPAIRED) — the artifacts carrying the timing facts it relies on — with no statement that either is impaired and no issue codes named."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Limb (b) of POL-NS-01 is not made out either. It requires the gps_trace to place the driver inside the pickup geofence 'at or before' the arrived event, and the earliest qualifying in-geofence fix on the trace is 23:42:21, which post-dates the trip record's 23:42:10 marker; the trace jumps from 23:39:15 to 23:42:21, so the 186-second gap swallows the moment of entry and no fix sits at the marker at all. The only route to limb (b) is to repair the timestamps using the 228-second device clock skew, and POL-NS-08(a) treats a skew over 90 seconds as an unsafe match between the arrived event and a GPS fix. POL-NS-01 is conjunctive and the party seeking the fee must carry every limb, so an unprovable limb (b) is a broken limb (b).",
      "side": "RIDER",
      "reason": "POL-GEN-02(1) non-disclosure. The claim relies on EV-GPS-00210 (weight_multiplier 0.25) and EV-TRIP-00210 (0.40), both impaired, without disclosing either impairment or naming any issue code."
    }
  ]
}
